    <heading level="1">Verbal</heading>
    <exercise number="1" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>What is a base $$b$$ logarithm? Discuss the meaning by interpreting each part of the equivalent equations $$b^{y} = x$$ and $$\log_{b}x = y$$ for $$b &gt; 0,b \neq 1.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>A logarithm is an exponent. Specifically, it is the exponent to which a base $$b b$$ is raised to produce a given value. In the expressions given, the base $$b b$$ has the same value. The exponent, $$y, y,$$ in the expression $$b y b y$$ can also be written as the logarithm, $$log b x, log b x,$$ and the value of $$x x$$ is the result of raising $$b b$$ to the power of $$y. y.$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="2" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>How is the logarithmic function $$f(x) = \log_{b}x$$ related to the exponential function $$g(x) = b^{x}?$$ What is the result of composing these two functions?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="3" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>How can the logarithmic equation $$\log_{b}x = y$$ be solved for $$x$$ using the properties of exponents?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Since the equation of a logarithm is equivalent to an exponential equation, the logarithm can be converted to the exponential equation $$b y =x, b y =x,$$ and then properties of exponents can be applied to solve for $$x. x.$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="4" has_solution="false">
